---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas20.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Recognition Criteria" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Grants Related to Income" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Grants Related to Assets – Presentation" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Non-Monetary Grants" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Mandatory Deferred Income Presentation – Indian Divergence" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Repayment of Government Grants" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 20 vs IAS 20 – Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 – CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Same income-matching philosophy as IAS 20, narrower presentation choice</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 20 shares IAS 20's core principle – government grants are recognised in P&amp;L on a SYSTEMATIC basis over the periods in which the entity recognises related costs, matching grants to the expenses/assets they are intended to compensate. Where Ind AS 20 genuinely diverges is PRESENTATION: it REMOVES IAS 20's option to deduct asset-related grants from the asset's carrying amount, mandating DEFERRED INCOME presentation only.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 20 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list"><span><strong>Comparability</strong> – mandating a SINGLE presentation approach (deferred income) is considered to enhance comparability WITHIN India, even though it narrows IAS 20's own flexibility</span><span><strong>Transparency</strong> – presenting the GROSS asset cost (rather than a net-of-grant amount) is considered by Indian standard-setters to provide clearer information about the asset's full cost</span></div>
        <div class="note">This is one of the FEW Ind AS standards with a genuine, explicit presentation-level divergence from its IFRS counterpart, comparable in character to Ind AS 1's format divergence.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Grant recognition – identical timing principle to IAS 20</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><div class="f-eq">Grants recognised in P&amp;L systematically over the periods matching the related costs they compensate</div></div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">IDENTICAL recognition TIMING principle to IAS 20 – the divergence is confined to PRESENTATION of asset-related grants.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 – SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 20 covers</div><div class="principle">Ind AS 20 applies to accounting for and disclosure of government grants, and disclosure of other forms of government assistance – IDENTICAL scope to IAS 20, EXCLUDING government participation in ownership (covered by Ind AS 109/32) and grants covered by Ind AS 41's fair value model.</div></div>
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Government grants</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 20 – assistance in the form of transfers of resources in return for past/future compliance with conditions.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Deferred income presentation</div><div class="glossary-def">Presenting an asset-related grant as a SEPARATE liability line item, released to P&amp;L over the asset's useful life – the ONLY presentation permitted under Ind AS 20.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 – RECOGNITION CRITERIA ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 20</div><div class="text-supporting">Grants are recognised only when there is REASONABLE ASSURANCE the entity will COMPLY with attached conditions and the grant will be RECEIVED – IDENTICAL recognition criteria to IAS 20.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 – GRANTS RELATED TO INCOME ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Grants Related to Income</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Identical to IAS 20</div><div class="text-supporting">Grants related to income are presented EITHER as a separate credit in P&amp;L, OR deducted from the related expense – IDENTICAL choice preserved under Ind AS 20 (this choice is NOT removed – only the asset-related grant deduction option is removed).</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 – GRANTS RELATED TO ASSETS (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Grants Related to Assets – Presentation (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The core divergence point, illustrated</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 1: Deferred income vs deduction method comparison</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian manufacturing company purchases machinery for ₹1,00,00,000 and receives a government capital subsidy of ₹20,00,000 under a state industrial promotion scheme, with the machinery having a 10-year useful life.</div><div class="wk-answer">
        Under IAS 20's DEDUCTION method (NOT available under Ind AS 20): the asset would be recognised at ₹80,00,000 net, with ₹8,00,000 annual depreciation.<br>
        Under Ind AS 20's MANDATORY DEFERRED INCOME method: the asset is recognised at the FULL ₹1,00,00,000, with ₹10,00,000 annual depreciation, while the ₹20,00,000 grant is recognised as DEFERRED INCOME (a liability), released to P&amp;L as ₹2,00,000 income per year over 10 years.<br>
        The NET P&amp;L IMPACT is IDENTICAL either way (₹8,00,000 net annual charge), but the GROSS PRESENTATION of the asset and the grant differs materially – illustrating Ind AS 20's mandatory presentation approach.
      </div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 – NON-MONETARY GRANTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Non-Monetary Grants</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 20</div><div class="text-supporting">Non-monetary grants (e.g. land, or other resources transferred at below-market terms) are usually recorded at FAIR VALUE – IDENTICAL to IAS 20, common for Indian state industrial land allotment schemes.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 – MANDATORY DEFERRED INCOME PRESENTATION ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Mandatory Deferred Income Presentation – Indian Divergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely important, well-documented Ind AS 20 divergence</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Unlike IAS 20, which offers preparers a CHOICE between deferred income presentation and netting the grant against the asset's carrying amount, Ind AS 20 removed this choice ENTIRELY for asset-related grants – the DEFERRED INCOME approach is the ONLY permitted presentation. This means Indian companies applying Ind AS 20 will ALWAYS show the GROSS cost of the subsidised asset on the balance sheet, with the grant appearing as a SEPARATE deferred income liability, unlike some IFRS preparers globally who might net the two together.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 – REPAYMENT ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Repayment of Government Grants</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Identical to IAS 20</div><div class="text-supporting">A grant becoming repayable is accounted for as a CHANGE IN ESTIMATE (per Ind AS 8) – first against any remaining deferred income balance, with any excess recognised immediately in P&amp;L – IDENTICAL to IAS 20.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 – DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical disclosure requirements to IAS 20</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy and presentation method adopted</span><span>Nature and extent of grants recognised</span><span>Unfulfilled conditions and contingencies attached to recognised grants</span></div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 – JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether "reasonable assurance" of compliance and receipt exists for grant recognition</span><span>Systematic basis for allocating grant income (typically matching depreciation pattern for asset-related grants)</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of non-monetary grants</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">Companies transitioning from OLD Indian GAAP (which permitted the deduction method) to Ind AS 20 needed to RECLASSIFY existing asset-related grants to the deferred income presentation, a genuinely notable one-time transition adjustment.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 – IND AS 20 vs IAS 20 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 20 vs IAS 20 – Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IAS 20</span><span>Ind AS 20</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Recognition criteria and timing</span><span>Standard model</span><span>IDENTICAL – no substantive divergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Grants related to assets – presentation</span><span>CHOICE: deferred income OR deduction from asset cost</span><span>DEFERRED INCOME ONLY – deduction method PROHIBITED</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Grants related to income – presentation</span><span>Choice preserved</span><span>IDENTICAL choice preserved</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Ind AS 20 is one of the RARE standards with a genuine, EXPLICIT presentation-level divergence from its IFRS counterpart – narrowing IAS 20's choice to a single mandatory method for asset-related grants, while leaving recognition timing and grants-related-to-income presentation unchanged.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 – STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Grant received (asset-related)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Recognised as deferred income liability, NOT netted against the asset</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Systematic release of deferred income</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Matches the depreciation pattern of the related asset</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Grant related to income</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Separate credit or deduction from related expense, identical to IAS 20</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 – RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 20</span><span>The global counterpart standard, converged in principle but diverging in mandatory presentation</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 16</span><span>Governs the gross asset cost against which the deferred grant income is matched</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 8</span><span>Governs the change-in-estimate treatment for grant repayment</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Deferred income</div><div class="glossary-def">The mandatory Ind AS 20 balance sheet presentation for asset-related grants, released to P&amp;L systematically.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Deduction method</div><div class="glossary-def">The IAS 20 alternative (netting the grant against asset cost) that Ind AS 20 does NOT permit.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 – GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Government grants", definition: "IDENTICAL definition to IAS 20 – assistance in the form of transfers of resources in return for past/future compliance with conditions." },
      { term: "Deferred income presentation", definition: "Presenting an asset-related grant as a SEPARATE liability line item, released to P&L over the asset's useful life – the ONLY presentation permitted under Ind AS 20." },
      { term: "Deferred income", definition: "The mandatory Ind AS 20 balance sheet presentation for asset-related grants, released to P&L systematically." },
      { term: "Deduction method", definition: "The IAS 20 alternative (netting the grant against asset cost) that Ind AS 20 does NOT permit." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
